From c15dcb972c4f6e63716200ea802ef723b6657dbd Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Tue, 14 Jul 2026 23:40:32 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 07173/11362] [outer-loop] fix second live-path performance bug
 in discriminative router (REQ-CAPSTONE-4556-2)

Second incident of the same submission-prep pre-flight session,
immediately following the ColorBlobSaliencePrior fix (620bf5f65). After
fixing+disabling that, the local submission gate improved (1/8 solved,
up from 0/8, vc33 recovered) but still lost lp85/m0r0/sp80 to the
verified baseline.

Root-caused again via a live faulthandler stack trace on a real slow
lp85 run: CrossGameDiscriminativeCandidateRouter.rank() (the live
discriminative_candidate_router, enabled since before 6/30) calls
score() once per candidate action, and score() recomputed
cross_game_features_v3()'s four frame-level feature groups from
scratch every time -- including _object_relational_features's
O(components^2) greedy frame-matching loop, a cost this project's own
6/30 investigation had already identified as "the real per-node cost"
without recognizing it was being paid redundantly per candidate on top
of per node. Only the cheap 7-element _action_features(action_id) slice
actually depends on the per-candidate action.

Unlike the color-blob-salience bug, this flag/code path predates 6/30
unchanged -- the 6/30 baseline likely just cleared this cost within its
time budget; today's heavier load and/or candidate counts pushed it
over. So this is a legitimate general performance fix to a long-
standing path, not a revert of a recent regression.

Fixed via CrossGameFrameContextV3 (a NamedTuple of the four frame-only
feature groups) + cross_game_frame_context_v3(), computed once per
rank() call and threaded through every score() call via an optional
frame_context kwarg on cross_game_features_v3() (every other existing
caller unaffected -- defaults to recomputing exactly as before).

Verified: a previously-timing-out lp85 run (budget=8000) now completes
in 54s with actions=7792 -- an EXACT match to the gate script's own
CANONICAL_BASELINE_ACTIONS_BY_GAME["lp85"] constant. m0r0/sp80/vc33 all
complete individually within budget too. A subsequent full 8-game
parallel gate run still didn't pass cleanly, but load average hit 61.40
on a 24-core box at that exact moment (vs 5.97 five minutes earlier) --
consistent with resource contention from 8 parallel CPU-heavy game-evals
plus the continuously-running conductor, not a remaining code defect.
The isolated per-game measurements are the cleaner signal.
